{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-320869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=3$ and $x=-2$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x-30=0,$ find the remaining solutions in the form $a\\pm b\\,\\text{i},$ where $b$ is **non-negative** number.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x-30.$ Since ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=-2$ are roots of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+2)={x}^{2}-x-6\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-x-6$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}-2x+5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-6 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x-30\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,-2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+7x-30 \\\\  & \\,{\\,-\\,2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+12x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,5{x}^{2}-5x-30 \\\\  & \\,{5{x}^{2}-5x-30}_{―} \\\\  & \\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-x-6)({x}^{2}-2x+5).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-2x+5=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+5 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+5-1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=3$ and $x=-2,$ are $x=1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-320869" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320869" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320869" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320869" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-17317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=-1$ and $x=4$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16=0,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-17317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16.$ Since ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ are roots of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-4)={x}^{2}-3x-4\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-3x-4$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-3x-4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,-12x-16\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-12x-16 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}-12x-16}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-3x-4)({x}^{2}+4).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-1,\\,4,\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-17317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-17317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-17317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=1\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=3\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=3\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-17317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\pm 2\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25=0.$ Given that $x=-1$ is a solution of multiplicity $2,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25.$ Since ${x}_{1,2}=-1$ is a root of multiplicity $2,$ according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNow, we can use long division to find the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+25 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,25{x}^{2}+50x+25 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{25{x}^{2}+50x+25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+2x+1)({x}^{2}+25).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+25=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-1,\\,-1,\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-106473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\pm 10\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm 25\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm 5\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106473" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pm 5\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation ${x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+20{x}^{2}-64x+64=0.$ Given that $x=2$ is a solution of multiplicity $2,$ find the remaining solutions in the form $\\pm b\\,\\text{i},$ where $b$ is **non-negative** number.\n\n$x=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}$\\text{i}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+20{x}^{2}-64x+64.$ Since ${x}_{1,2}=2$ is a root of multiplicity $2,$ according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}={x}^{2}-4x+4\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNow, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-4x+4$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+16 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-4x+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+20{x}^{2}-64x+64\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,16{x}^{2}-64x+64 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{16{x}^{2}-64x+64}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+4)({x}^{2}+16).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+16=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from ${x}_{1,2}=2,$ are $x=\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-320894" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320894" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation ${x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27=0.$ Given that $x=-3$ is a solution of multiplicity $2,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27.$ Since ${x}_{1,2}=-3$ is a root of multiplicity $2,$ according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}={x}^{2}+6x+9\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNow, we can use long division to find the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+6x+9\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27\\,\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{2}+18x+27 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3{x}^{2}+18x+27}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x)=({x}^{2}+6x+9)({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+3=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=-3,\\,-3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-108275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}"] ["math" "1\\pm 9\\text{i}"] ["math" "2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}"] ["math" "3\\pm 5\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=-2-5\\text{i}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-122x+377=0,$ find the remaining solutions in the form $a\\pm b\\,\\text{i},$ where $b$ is **non-negative** number.\n\n$x=-2\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-3}}$\\text{i}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-122x+377.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-2-5\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-2-5\\text{i}}=-2+5\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(-2+5\\text{i}))(x-(-2-5\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-2+5\\text{i}))(x-(-2-5\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(-2-5\\text{i})x-(-2+5\\text{i})x+(-2-5\\text{i})(-2+5\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+((-2)^{2}-(5\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+4x+29$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}-6x+13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+29 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-122x+377\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+29{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,-6{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-122x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}-174x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,13{x}^{2}+52x+377 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{13{x}^{2}+52x+377}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+4x+29)({x}^{2}-6x+13).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-6x+13=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+13 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+9)+13-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=-2-5\\text{i},$ are\n$x=-2+5\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-320934" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b0945