\\pm $\n{{field-3}}$\\text{i}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-275146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}-72x+153.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=4-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}}=4+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(4+\\text{i}))(x-(4-\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(4+\\text{i}))(x-(4-\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(4-\\text{i})x-(4+\\text{i})x+(4-\\text{i})(4+\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+({4}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-8x+17$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+17 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}-72x+153\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+17{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,9{x}^{2}-72x+153 \\\\  & \\,{9{x}^{2}-72x+153}_{―} \\\\  & \\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-8x+17)({x}^{2}+9).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+9=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=4-\\text{i},$ are\n$x=4+1\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=0\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-275146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-275146" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-275146" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-275146" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-275146" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-275146" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-275146" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=3-4\\,\\text{i}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+76x+100=0,$ find the remaining solutions.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i},$ \n and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+76x+100.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-4\\,\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-4\\,\\text{i}}=3+4\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, $(x-(3-4\\,\\text{i}))(x-(3+4\\,\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-4\\,\\text{i}))(x-(3+4\\,\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(3+4\\,\\text{i})x-(3-4\\,\\text{i})x+(3-4\\,\\text{i})(3+4\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+25$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+25 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+76x+100\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}+76x \\\\  & \\,{\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+100x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-24x+100 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}-24x+100}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+25)({x}^{2}+4x+4).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4x+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=3-4\\,\\text{i},$ are\n$x=3+4\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-320923" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40=0$ given that $x=1+3\\text{i}$ is a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i})x-(1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-2x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-2x+10)({x}^{2}+4).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-106479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 4\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 6\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 8\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm \\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-106479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25=0$ given that $x=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}$ is a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}}=-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x-(-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x-(-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})) & =(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-(\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+5$ using long division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-6x+5\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-6{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-30x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}-30x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5{x}^{2}+25 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5{x}^{2}+25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+5)({x}^{2}-6x+5).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-6x+5=0:$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,1,\\,5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "550ed929f0c47bb4c4a9143752cefcdac748d94303ff3ed34a2caadff9f17650"} {"question" "q-108344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,1,\\,5"] ["math" "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,2,\\,3"] ["math" "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,2,\\,5"] ["math" "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2"] ["math" "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,\\pm 5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "q-108344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,1,\\,5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b276e14b6e23ca51b09453d6b0b882a4a7a2894acf4ec588c8302fc4d32d42d"} {"question" "e-8802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are solutions to the equation $x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13 = 0,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/example-8802.json" "file_sha256" "e5d6e282b222e045bbd85a826c53953b74a380fe81d7851c3eedc6b8ca322609"} {"question" "e-8802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13.$ Since $x_1=-1$ and $x_2=1$ are roots of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2-1$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-6x+13 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+6x-13\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-6{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+6x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+6x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,13{x}^{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,-13 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,13{x}^{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,-13}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2-1)(x^2-6x+13).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $x^2-6x+13=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+13 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+13 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\pm 1$ and $x=3\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/example-8802.json" "file_sha256" "e5d6e282b222e045bbd85a826c53953b74a380fe81d7851c3eedc6b8ca322609"} {"question" "e-5797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=2\\textrm{i}$ is a solution to the quartic equation $x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36 = 0$, find all of the solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/example-5797.json" "file_sha256" "a561fc0dc29ccef3740159bd0cd8730061f4be169a6365dc0645717a27da5ef2"} {"question" "e-5797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x) = x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and $x_1=2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{2\\textrm{i}}=-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$\\big(x-2\\text{i}\\big)\\big(x-(-2\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i})) & =(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2+4$ using long division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+9 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+13{x}^{2}+36\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,9{x}^{2}+36 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{9{x}^{2}+36}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2+4)(x^2+9).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve $x^2+9 = 0{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=\\pm 2\\textrm{i}, \\, \\pm 3 \\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066689/example-5797.json" "file_sha256" "a561fc0dc29ccef3740159bd0cd8730061f4be169a6365dc0645717a27da5ef2"}] "usage" {"e-8802" [] "e-5797" []} "basis" 2201 "retractions" [[17592186175735 :question/problem "Given that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are solutions to the equation $x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13 = 0$, find the remaining solutions."] [17592186175735 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13\n$$\n\nSince $x_1=-1$ and $x_2=1$ are roots of $f(x)$, then according to the factor theorem,\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x)$.\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2-1$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 6x + 13; x^{2} - 1x^{4} - 6x^{3} + 12x^{2} + 6x - 13; x^{4} - x^{2}_(―); - 6x^{3} + 13x^{2} + 6x; - 6x^{3} + 6x_(―); x^{4} + 313x^{2} - 13; x^{4} + 313x^{2} - 13_(―); x^{4} + 3x^{3} - 5x^{2} + 150\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x^2-1)(x^2-6x+13)\n$$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve\n\n$$\nx^2-6x+13=0\n$$\n\nby completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x + 3^{2}) - 3^{2} + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nx - 3 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nx &= 3 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\pm 1$ and\n\n$$\nx=3\\pm 2\\textrm{i}\n$$"] [17592186175709 :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx_1=2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nis a root of $f(x)$, the complex conjugate\n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{2\\textrm{i}}=-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x)$.\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem,\n$\\big(x-2\\text{i}\\big)\\big(x-(-2\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x)$. Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2\\textrm{i})(x - (-2\\textrm{i})) &= (x - 2\\textrm{i})(x + 2\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - (2\\textrm{i})^{2}) \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2+4$ using long division.\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 9; x^{2} + 4x^{4} + 13x^{2} + 36; x^{4} + 4x^{2}_(―); 2x^{4} +9x^{2} + 36; 2x^{4} +9x^{2} + 36_(―); 2x^{4} + 2x^{2} +00\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x^2+4)(x^2+9)\n$$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve\n\n$$\nx^2+9 = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nx &= ± 3\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2\\textrm{i}, \\, \\pm 3 \\textrm{i}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
